From: djnice <djnice77@gmail.com>
Subject: bdj: drawing a null image is a no-op, not a NullPointerException

java.awt.Graphics.drawImage() with a null image draws nothing: the JDK's
SunGraphics2D returns true straight away, and BD-J menu frameworks rely on
it. Every drawImage() overload of BDGraphicsBase funnels into drawImageN(),
where a null image fails all the instanceof tests and reaches the
"unsupported image type" branch, whose img.getClass() throws a
NullPointerException back into the Xlet.

Paramount's "radius" menu framework (e.g. Planes, Trains and Automobiles
UHD) builds its sprites from composite PNGs under BDMV/JAR/00001 and keeps
a sprite whose image did not load with a null image, drawing it anyway.
The NPE thrown from inside the paint pass of onMediaPresented() aborts the
menu's setup: the language selector is on screen, but no button is
focused/highlighted and the remote cannot select anything, so the disc
never gets past it.

Return true for a null image before anything else, as the JDK does.
--- a/src/libbluray/bdj/java/java/awt/BDGraphicsBase.java
+++ b/src/libbluray/bdj/java/java/awt/BDGraphicsBase.java
@@ -1297,6 +1297,10 @@
         boolean flipX, boolean flipY,
         Color bg, ImageObserver observer) {
 
+        /* java.awt.Graphics: a null image draws nothing (the JDK returns true) */
+        if (img == null)
+            return true;
+
         if ((sw == 0) || (sh == 0) || (dw == 0) || (dh == 0))
             return false;
 
